410→                           Erdős originally asked Mahler whether there are infinitely many pairs of consecutive powerful numbers, but Mahler immediately observed that the answer is yes from the infinitely many solutions to the Pell equation $x^2=8y^2+1$.  <br><br>Note that $8$ is 3-full and $9$ is 2-full. Erdős and Graham asked if this is the only pair of such consecutive integers. Stephan has observed that $12167=23^3$ and $12168=2^33^213^2$ (a pair already known to Golomb <a href="#cite-Go70-366" onclick="event.preventDefault();addNewBox('Go70','366'); return false;">[Go70]</a>) is another example, but (by <a href="https://oeis.org/A060355" rel="nofollow noopener noreferrer ugc" target="_blank">OEIS A060355</a>) there are no other examples for $n&lt;10^{22}$.<br><br>In <a href="#cite-Er76d-366" onclick="event.preventDefault();addNewBox('Er76d','366'); return false;">[Er76d]</a> Erdős asks the weaker question of whether there are any consecutive pairs of $3$-full integers (which is also discussed in problem B16 of Guy's collection <a href="#cite-Gu04-366" onclick="event.preventDefault();addNewBox('Gu04','366'); return false;">[Gu04]</a>).
